.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt CHOWN 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm chown ,
.Nm lchown ,
.Nm chown32 ,
.Nm lchown32 ,
.Nm fchown ,
.Nm fchown32 ,
.Nm fchownat
.Nd change file ownership
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn chown "const char *path" "uid_t owner" "gid_t group"
.Ft int
.Fn lchown "const char *path" "uid_t owner" "gid_t group"
.Ft int
.Fn fchown "int fd" "uid_t owner" "gid_t group"
.Ft int
.Fn fchownat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "uid_t owner" "gid_t group" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These calls change a file's owner and group.
An
.Fa owner
of
.Li (uid_t)-1
leaves the owner unchanged; a
.Fa group
of
.Li (gid_t)-1
leaves the group unchanged.
.Pp
.Fn chown
and
.Fn lchown
resolve
.Fa path
relative to the current working directory unless it is absolute.
.Fn chown
follows a final symbolic link on a file system that has them, and
.Fn lchown
changes the link itself, as on Linux.
The KRFFS boot root has no symbolic links, so there the two are the
same.
A path that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
names a directory: both calls follow a final symbolic link then, and
anything but a directory returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux.
The
.Pa /proc
links have fixed ownership, which
.Fn lchown
reaches; a
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd/ Ns Ar n
link is owned by its process, and
.Fn lchown
of it, or
.Fn fchownat
with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
of an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor of it, stores the IDs in the link inode.
Either asks the link's owner: a new owner, even the same one, takes the
owner asking for itself or a privileged caller, and a new group the owner
naming the link's group or one of its own, or a privileged caller, as
Linux's
.Fn chown_ok
and
.Fn chgrp_ok
judge.
.Fn lchown
looks the link up, and returns
.Er ENOENT
once the descriptor is closed; an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor holds the link and judges the owner its open gave it, as Linux
does.
.Fn chown
follows
.Pa /proc/self
to a process directory, whose ownership is fixed too, follows a
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd/ Ns Ar n
link to what the descriptor is open on, a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
included, and follows
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /exe
to the program's file itself, wherever it is now, as Linux does, and
changes that file: a privileged
.Fn chown
of
.Pa /proc/self/exe
changes the program's owner on a writable file system.
.Fn fchown
uses the open descriptor
.Fa fd ;
of a named FIFO, or of a device opened through a device node of tmpfs or
ext2, it changes that node.
The kernel's
.Fn fchown
returns
.Er EBADF
for one opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
as Linux does, and for a pseudo-terminal slave descriptor with no live
terminal.
Musl's
.Fn fchown
retries that error, when
.Dv F_GETFD
finds the descriptor open, as a
.Fn chown
of
.Pa /proc/self/fd/N ,
which the kernel follows to the descriptor's file.
.Pp
.Fn fchownat
resolves a relative
.Fa path
from the directory descriptor
.Fa dirfd ,
or from the current working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
.Fa flags
may have two bits, and any other returns
.Er EINVAL
before the path is read.
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
changes a final symbolic link itself, as
.Fn lchown
does.
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
with an empty path changes the file
.Fa dirfd
refers to, one opened with
.Dv O_PATH
included, by the rules of
.Fn fchown ,
or the current working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
An empty path without
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
returns
.Er ENOENT ,
and a null one
.Er EFAULT .
.Ss Ownership rules
A caller with
.Dv CAP_CHOWN
over the inode may set either ID.
Otherwise, only the owner, judged by filesystem user ID, may supply an ID.
The owner may name itself as owner, and the file's group or one of its
own groups as group.
These checks also apply to an ID equal to the file's current ID.
Any other request is
.Er EPERM ,
as Linux's
.Fn chown_ok
and
.Fn chgrp_ok
judge it.
-1 leaves the corresponding ID.
If neither ID is supplied, ownership permission is needed only when
the call clears a set-id bit.
Path resolution still requires search permission on ancestor directories.
.Pp
KRFFS and ext2 store ownership per file.
On a writable mounted KRFFS volume, an otherwise permitted change to its
root returns success without storing the IDs.
FAT32 and exFAT report the mount's owner and group; a change to either
is refused.
A pseudo-terminal slave under
.Pa /dev/pts
stores its own owner and group, by the same rules.
A directory or link made under
.Pa /dev
stores its owner and group as a file does, and so does a device under
.Pa /dev ,
whose owner and group the next open checks, as udev sets those of Linux's
devtmpfs nodes; a device registered anew starts as root's.
A
.Pa /proc/PID/fd/N
link named without following it, or by an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor opened with
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW ,
is judged by the link's own owner, and a change stays with the link until
the next lookup of its path gives it its process's owner again, as on
Linux.
Other synthetic objects under
.Pa /dev ,
.Pa /proc
and
.Pa /sys ,
and other descriptor kinds, keep the owner and group
.Xr stat 2
gives them: a request the rules allow succeeds when it names those IDs
or -1, and any other is
.Er EPERM .
A
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc
node named by a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
follows the rules of its path.
On Linux a pipe or a socket has an owner of its own, which
.Fn fchown
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
change; here both keep the owner
.Xr fstat 2
gives them.
.Pp
A successful chown sets the change time, even when both IDs are -1.
Modification time stays as it was.
On a nondirectory, chown clears set-user-ID.
It clears set-group-ID
if group execute is set or if the caller is outside the file's group and
lacks
.Dv CAP_FSETID
over the inode.
A mode change requires the file's owner or
.Dv CAP_FOWNER .
Inotify reports a supplied ID or a mode change.
.Ss System call numbers
The i386 numbers are 182 for
.Nm chown ,
16 for
.Nm lchown ,
212 for
.Nm chown32 ,
198 for
.Nm lchown32 ,
95 for
.Nm fchown ,
207 for
.Nm fchown32 ,
and 298 for
.Nm fchownat .
On the i686 kernel each older number and its 32-bit variant use the same
handler and 32-bit ID arguments.
On the x86-64 kernel, for a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
which
.Xr ia32 7
describes, the older numbers take 16-bit IDs, as on Linux: an argument's
low 16 bits, with the 16-bit -1 leaving the ID as it is.
Musl uses the 32-bit variants for
.Fn chown ,
.Fn lchown ,
and
.Fn fchown ;
it declares no separate functions with the
.Li 32
suffix.
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm chown
(92),
.Nm fchown
(93),
.Nm lchown
(94) and
.Nm fchownat
(260) take 32-bit IDs, and there are no 32-suffixed calls.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/ownprobe.c
changes
.Pa /own.txt
to owner and group 1000, then changes its group to 2000 through the open
descriptor.
After dropping privilege, it checks that the owner can change the group
to supplementary group 3000 and that group 4000 is refused.
.Pp
.Pa tests/musl/lchownmusl.c
changes a file through
.Fn lchown
and each form of
.Fn fchownat ,
an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor and the working directory among them, and asks for each
refusal of the flags.
Given a directory on an ext2 volume, as
.Ic tests/run ext2
gives it
.Pa /tmp
on an ext2 root, it also requires that
.Fn lchown
and
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
change a symbolic link and leave its target alone, that
.Fn chown
changes the target, and that a dangling link can be changed.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission was denied on an ancestor directory, or a final
symbolic link the call would follow is in a sticky directory others may
write and neither the caller nor the directory's owner owns it, root
included
.Pq Xr symlink 2 .
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open; a relative
.Fn fchownat
path uses an invalid or stale directory descriptor; or
.Fn fchownat
with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
and an empty path was given a
.Fa dirfd
that is not open.
The kernel's
.Fn fchown ,
and
.Fn fchownat
with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
also return it for a pseudo-terminal slave descriptor with no live
terminal, and
.Fn fchown
for a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH ;
musl's
.Fn fchown
retries the error through
.Pa /proc/self/fd/N ,
as the description says.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The path cannot be read from user memory.
The path forms also return this error if there is no current process.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn fchownat
was given a flag other than
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
an ID is one the caller's user namespace does not map, which is judged
after the path resolves and after the read-only check, as Linux judges it,
or the file system cannot supply the required attributes.
.It Bq Er EIO
File system metadata could not be read or written, or the device was removed.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The path, its expansion relative to a directory, a component, or a
symbolic-link expansion is too long.
The copied path and its directory prefix must fit 4096 bytes,
including the terminating null byte.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The path is empty, without
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH
for
.Fn fchownat ,
or a required path component does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The file system could not allocate storage needed to resolve the path.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A path component used as a directory is not a directory,
.Fa dirfd
is not a directory descriptor for a relative path, or a path that ends
in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
reaches something else.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The caller lacks permission to make the ownership change, the object
has fixed ownership, or the file is immutable or append-only.
.It Bq Er EROFS
The file's mount, or the mount a descriptor was opened through, is
read-only, made so alone or over a read-only file system, the halted
KRFFS root among them: checked once the path resolves and before any
other check, even for a request that changes neither ID, as Linux takes
the mount for writing first.
.Pa /sys
is mounted read-only though its attributes take writes, and gives none.
A file system without an attribute-setting operation gives it too.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
.Fn fchown
has no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr stat 2
